{"url": "https://www.ckwri.tamuk.edu/current-openings/strategies-and-technologies-enhance-habitat-restoration-degraded-rangelands", "final_url": "https://www.ckwri.tamuk.edu/current-openings/strategies-and-technologies-enhance-habitat-restoration-degraded-rangelands", "status": 200, "method": "http", "fetched": "2026-11-14", "text": "Strategies and technologies to enhance habitat restoration of degraded rangelands | Caesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute\nSkip to main content\nSearch\nGlobal Menu\nEnews Sign up\nTeam Portal\nMenu\nMain navigation\nAbout\nCaesar Kleberg\nCaesar Kleberg Foundation\nCaesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute\nAdvisory Board\nDevelopment Team\nScience Team\nOperations Team\nGraduate Students\nProspective Students\nResearch Programs\nPatton Center For Deer Research\nFeline Research Program\nFire Ecology\nHabitat Management, Restoration & Research\nLandscape Ecology\nLivestock & Wildlife Relationships\nMolecular Genetics\nQuail Associates 2.0\nHenry Hamman Program for Hill Country Conservation and Management\nNorth Texas research Program\nRichard M. Kleberg Jr Center for Quail Research\nSouth Texas Natives\nTexas Native Seeds Program (TNS)\nWaterfowl and Wetland Birds\nWildlife Photography Program\nWildlife Diseases, Parasitology and Toxicology Research Program\nWildlife Center & Facilities\nAll Facilities\nTio and Janell Kleberg Wildlife Research Park\nCKWRI Ocelot Conservation Facility\nGiving\nMake a Donation\nCurrent Named Endowments\nNamed Giving Opportunities\nDevelopment Team\nGiving Forms\nPublications\nNews & Events\nMedia\nA Talk on the Wild Side Podcast\nSomewhere West of Wall Street\nWest of Texas\nEmployment\nContact\nBreadcrumb\nStrategies and Technologies To Enhance Habitat Restoration of Degraded Rangelands\nStrategies and technologies to enhance habitat restoration of degraded rangelands\nPosition Description\nWe are seeking a highly motivated M.S. or Ph.D. student to work on an exciting project at the intersection of restoration ecology, geospatial data science and ecological modeling. The graduate student selected will conduct research on habitat restoration of degraded rangelands (e.g., energy extraction, woody plant encroachment, and rangeland conversion). Project goals include using native species restoration, prescribed fire, and adaptive grazing to reduce the risks and impacts of drought and invasive plant species, as well as to address issues related to woody encroachment to enhance wildlife habitat in Texas. The successful candidate will pursue an M.S. or Ph.D. (depending on qualifications) degree in Range and Wildlife Management (M.S.) or Wildlife and Rangeland Sciences (Ph.D.) with the Caesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute, Department of Rangeland and Wildlife Sciences, Texas A&M University-Kingsville. Additional duties include publication of research results; presentations at professional conferences; assisting with other projects and other duties as assigned.\nRequired Qualifications\nB.S. (for M.S. position) or M.S. (for Ph.D.) in rangeland science, plant ecology, botany, environmental science, geography or closely related field.\nExcellent written and verbal communication skills in English.\nStrong quantitative and problem-solving skills.\nStrong work ethic, ability to work independently and as a productive member of a research team.\nAbility to work under adverse field conditions (hot humid environment).\nPreferred Qualifications\nBackground in plant identification, and experience basic field ecology and surveying vegetation.\nExperience with geospatial technologies such as GPS and Geographic Information Systems (GIS).\nFamiliarity with geospatial data (climate, vegetation, soil), remote sensing data products.\nA strong interest in ecological modeling and data science.\nAbility and willingness to drive vehicle and lead field survey in difficult terrain/weather.\nStipend/Salary\n$2,000/month for M.S., $2,500/month for Ph.D., plus benefits (medical package available after completion of waiting period). Nonresident tuition is waived (resident tuition and fees apply). Partial resident tuition fee assistance may be provided based on availability of funds.\nStart Date\nJanuary, 2027 or earliest time thereafter.\nApplication Deadline\nThe position is open and will remain open until an appropriate candidate is found.\nTo Apply\nApplication packet should consist of 1) Current CV, 2) two writing samples, 3) cover letter outlining research interests (2-3 pages max), 4) unofficial undergraduate/graduate transcripts, and 5) contact information of up to three professional references as a single PDF file titled: CKWRI-RRE-2026-[LastName_Firstname].pdf and use \u2018CKWRI-RRE-2026\u2019 as subject line for the email to Dr. Weimin Xi (weimin.xi@tamuk.edu).\nFor questions regarding this position: Dr. Weimin Xi, Professor, Frances and Peter Swenson Endowed Chair in Rangeland Restoration Ecology, Caesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute, Texas A&M University-Kingsville. Email: weimin.xi@tamuk.edu; Research Lab: https://drxilab.netlify.app/\nTexas law requires that males 18 through 25 show proof of compliance with Federal Selective Service law in order to be eligible for employment. Selected candidate must pass a pre-employment background investigation to be hired for this position. EEO/AA/ADA\nDonate to\nCKWRI\nLearn more about how you can impact future of wildlife and wildlife habitat today\nDonate now\nMain navigation\nAbout\nCaesar Kleberg\nCaesar Kleberg Foundation\nCaesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute\nAdvisory Board\nDevelopment Team\nScience Team\nOperations Team\nGraduate Students\nProspective Students\nResearch Programs\nPatton Center For Deer Research\nFeline Research Program\nFire Ecology\nHabitat Management, Restoration & Research\nLandscape Ecology\nLivestock & Wildlife Relationships\nMolecular Genetics\nQuail Associates 2.0\nHenry Hamman Program for Hill Country Conservation and Management\nNorth Texas research Progr